WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.058
Picture writing a letter to a friend.

2
00:00:05.058 --> 00:00:10.476
You write three pages, fold them into an envelope, seal it, and hand it to a courier.

3
00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:13.472
Your friend opens it and reads all three pages.

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.166
Nobody worries that the courier might leave while page three is still on your desk.

5
00:00:19.166 --> 00:00:22.277
If one ever did, you'd simply find a better courier.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.590
Programs do this between threads all day.

7
00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.812
A thread is one path of work running beside others.

8
00:00:28.812 --> 00:00:33.992
Our shop keeps its card fees on a rate card: a currency, and a fee per card brand.

9
00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:40.563
A loader thread builds a new card when fees change, and request threads, which handle payments, read it.

10
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:44.868
The loader stores the new RateCard in a shared field, current.

11
00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:46.223
Three things happen.

12
00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:52.592
The JVM, the program that runs Java code, makes the object with every field blank: null, or 0.

13
00:00:52.592 --> 00:00:55.235
The constructor writes the real values.

14
00:00:55.235 --> 00:01:00.113
Then the reference, the value pointing at the object, goes into current.

15
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:05.006
In the letter's terms, the card is the envelope, and its fields are the pages.

16
00:01:05.006 --> 00:01:07.025
The constructor writes the pages.

17
00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:09.839
Storing the reference hands the envelope over.

18
00:01:09.839 --> 00:01:12.164
And the request thread is your friend.

19
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:15.576
So here's a question that feels too obvious to ask.

20
00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:18.950
The constructor finished before the card was stored.

21
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.611
So whoever finds the card finds it whole.

22
00:01:21.611 --> 00:01:22.000
Right?

23
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:23.742
Let's test that.

24
00:01:23.742 --> 00:01:28.521
A request thread reads current, finds a card, and reads its currency.

25
00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:29.629
What can it get?

26
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.658
It can get null.

27
00:01:33.658 --> 00:01:37.958
A real rate card, with a blank page where the currency should be.

28
00:01:37.958 --> 00:01:42.391
Or a VISA fee of 0, which the card company won't find funny at all.

29
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:43.355
Why?

30
00:01:43.355 --> 00:01:50.932
A write is promised to another thread only when a happens-before edge links them, like the cashier's key from the bank.

31
00:01:50.932 --> 00:01:55.771
Nothing links these two threads, so two layers are free to change the order.

32
00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:02.648
The JIT, the compiler inside the JVM, may move the store of the reference ahead of the constructor's stores.

33
00:02:02.648 --> 00:02:09.709
And the processor may show its stores to its other cores, the parts that each run a thread, in a different order.

34
00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:12.645
ARM processors do this more than Intel and AMD.

35
00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:15.539
So the reader sees the store to current first.

36
00:02:15.539 --> 00:02:19.578
It finds a card that isn't null, reads the currency, and gets null.

37
00:02:19.578 --> 00:02:22.109
The real currency shows up a moment later.

38
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:32.300
The courier delivered the envelope before the pages were inside, and there's no better courier to hire: it's the JIT and the processor, built into every machine.

39
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.614
Where the picture breaks: a real courier can't carry unwritten pages.

40
00:02:36.614 --> 00:02:39.491
The JIT just moves your store, without a word.

41
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.054
Is it real?

42
00:02:41.054 --> 00:02:47.649
The team that builds Java has a stress tester that runs tiny tests millions of times.

43
00:02:47.649 --> 00:02:54.788
One test publishes an object with four fields, 1, 2, 3 and 4, and another thread reads them.

44
00:02:54.788 --> 00:03:00.685
On an Intel or AMD machine, in 219 million tries, how often are pages blank?

45
00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.568
Here's the recorded run.

46
00:03:05.568 --> 00:03:09.450
Most readers saw no object yet, or the whole one.

47
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:13.095
But 18,306 times, some pages were still blank.

48
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.569
About one in 12,000.

49
00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:22.988
Rare, but a busy shop takes a lot of card payments, and rare bugs have terrible manners: they wait until you're on holiday.

50
00:03:22.988 --> 00:03:27.300
On ARM, the test saw almost every mix of zeros and real values.

51
00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:28.730
But wait.

52
00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:39.334
In the office, x86 processors, the Intel and AMD kind, kept their writes in order: notes left each outbox tray in the order they went in.

53
00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:40.960
So who swapped these?

54
00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:42.237
The test checks.

55
00:03:42.237 --> 00:03:49.845
When it makes the JIT keep those writes in order, the half-built objects vanish on x86, and stay on ARM.

56
00:03:49.845 --> 00:03:51.821
The processor has an alibi.

57
00:03:51.821 --> 00:03:54.235
On x86, the JIT moved the stores.

58
00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:55.843
Now the names.

59
00:03:55.843 --> 00:04:00.473
Storing a reference where another thread can find it is called publishing.

60
00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:06.856
An object seen before its fields is a partially constructed object, and no line you wrote creates one.

61
00:04:06.856 --> 00:04:09.735
The rule: never publish through a plain field.

62
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:11.685
So seal the envelope.

63
00:04:11.685 --> 00:04:16.143
Make current volatile, a field whose reads always see the latest write.

64
00:04:16.143 --> 00:04:20.977
The volatile write is the seal: everything written before it travels with it.

65
00:04:20.977 --> 00:04:25.497
A reader who reads the same field breaks the seal, and finds every page.

66
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.033
A lock, a static initializer, or a concurrent collection seal it too.

67
00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:34.851
Like the cashier's key, a seal only counts if the reader uses the same one.

68
00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:40.823
Final fields add one more promise: pages glued into the envelope at the factory.

69
00:04:40.823 --> 00:04:45.948
Drop it in a puddle, lose it under the sofa, and those pages are still inside.

70
00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:55.100
A thread that sees the object after its constructor finished sees its final fields as the constructor left them, even with no seal.

71
00:04:55.100 --> 00:05:01.099
With all four fields final, the test found not one half-built object, on x86 or on ARM.

72
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.078
But glue doesn't post letters.

73
00:05:03.078 --> 00:05:12.654
A reader looping on a plain field, waiting for the first card, may copy it onto its notepad once, like the stop flag in the office, and never look again.

74
00:05:12.654 --> 00:05:16.409
So make the data final, and still publish it through a seal.

75
00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:23.092
In code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right: final fields, and a volatile current.

76
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.180
So how can a reader find the card without its currency?

77
00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.393
Other threads don't read your lines.

78
00:05:29.393 --> 00:05:34.432
They see writes, in whatever order they arrive, and a reference is one more write.

79
00:05:34.432 --> 00:05:35.539
Seal the envelope.

80
00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:36.435
Quick check.

81
00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:40.469
Every field in RateCard is final, but current is a plain field.

82
00:05:40.469 --> 00:05:42.582
Can a reader see a null currency?

83
00:05:42.582 --> 00:05:45.592
And can a waiting reader miss the card forever?
